WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 10.9 · Legal, Compliance, Policies & Risk
Writing usable procedures

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Write procedures now, even for tasks only you do, so others can take them over as you grow.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:27.763
A procedure helps someone carry out a real task the same way every time. Every important policy needs at least one procedure showing how to apply it, even if only you do the task today.

4
00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:28.763
Four parts of a procedure

5
00:00:28.763 --> 00:00:40.093
Trigger and owner: What starts the task, and who is responsible for it. For example: When the youth centre refers a new trainee, the founder who leads trainee support starts onboarding.

6
00:00:40.093 --> 00:00:50.681
Steps: The actions in order, written in plain language. For example: Send the code of conduct, collect the signed form, book a first shift with a mentor.

7
00:00:50.681 --> 00:00:59.789
Records: What is written down or kept as evidence. For example: The signed form is filed in the trainee folder within a week.

8
00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:12.319
Exceptions: What to do when things do not go as planned. For example: If a trainee is 17, consent from a parent or carer comes before the first shift.

9
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:13.319
Why write it down now

10
00:01:13.319 --> 00:01:18.252
1. Others will take over your tasks as you grow.

11
00:01:18.252 --> 00:01:23.926
2. The same steps every time mean fewer mistakes and fairer treatment.

12
00:01:23.926 --> 00:01:30.600
3. Funders and insurers may ask how you actually apply your policies.

13
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:31.600
How you will test it

14
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.274
1. Ask someone who did not write it to walk through it.

15
00:01:37.274 --> 00:01:43.319
2. Note where they pause, guess or need permission, and fix those points.

16
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:49.993
3. Add urgent and safeguarding steps based on local advice and services.

17
00:01:50.393 --> 00:01:50.993
What to avoid

18
00:01:50.993 --> 00:01:56.667
Keeping procedures in your head because you do the task yourself.

19
00:01:56.667 --> 00:02:01.230
Treating a procedure as ready without testing it.

20
00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:06.422
Writing emergency instructions without qualified local advice.

21
00:02:07.022 --> 00:02:08.822
You draft one priority procedure for your launch and plan its test.

22
00:02:08.822 --> 00:02:12.822
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:14.322 --> 00:02:17.422
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
